<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Brandenburg Concertos</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1721</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> Concertos are six instrumental works by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-sebastian-bach">Johann Sebastian Bach</a>, among the best-known compositions of the baroque. Bach probably wrote most of them while he was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapellmeister">Kapellmeister</a> at the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kothen">Köthen</a>, and in 1721 he presented a fair copy to Christian Ludwig, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margrave-of-brandenburg">Margrave of Brandenburg</a>, from whom they take their name. Each concerto uses a different combination of solo instruments.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/brandenburg-concertos.jpg" alt="Brandenburg Concertos" width="320" height="193" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: J.S. Bach or his writer · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Title_page_of_Brandenburg_Concertos.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
